    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1062">

                <pb facs="1214" n="1102" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg1102"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.5034 (China) Thrift Corp./40: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-10-10T11:00:00+08:00">October 10, 1935—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 10—7 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>42. Embassy’s 37, October 8, noon and Shanghai’s 592, October 9, 7 p.m.<anchor
                        n="10" xml:id="d1062-f1"/> Following from Harbin: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“October 9, 3 p.m. My October 7, 7 p.m. Thriftcor Bank has received a
                            telegram from H. D. Roger, the bank’s attorney and vice president in
                            Shanghai, stating that the United States Court for China has refused
                            American jurisdiction, that Shanghai liquidation must be separate from
                            that in Manchuria and that each liquidation must be under local law.</p>
                        <pb facs="1215" n="1103" xml:id="pg1103"/>
                        <p>If this information is officially confirmed I believe that I should issue
                            a simple announcement locally that American authorities have declined to
                            assume jurisdiction in order to prevent impression that local
                            authorities have assumed jurisdiction in derogation of American
                            extraterritorial rights. I suggest approval of issuing such a statement
                            if Roger’s message is officially confirmed.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Embassy has repeated Adams’ telegram to Shanghai for the Judge of the United
                    States Court for China with the following comments. “Do you authorize Adams to
                    issue an announcement that the American authorities have declined to assume
                    jurisdiction over the Thriftcor Bank? Please telegraph Adams directly through
                    the Consul General at Shanghai notifying the Embassy, or if you prefer I will
                    instruct Adams if you concur in the proposal to issue the announcement in
                    question and will so inform me by telegraph.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
